====Usage notes====
* In the United States and Canada, {{m|en||toilet}} refers most directly to fixtures for containing or removing human waste. As such, although {{m|en||toilet}} was originally a euphemism itself, its use to describe the place where the toilets are located (e.g., "Where is the toilet?") is now considered somewhat indiscreet; instead, it is more common to employ other euphemisms such as {{m|en|bathroom}}, {{m|en|restroom}}, or {{m|en|WC}}.  Until the late 19th century, {{m|en||toilet}} referred solely to personal grooming, including bathing and hair care. This still appears in {{m|en|toiletries}} and in various set phrases, such as {{m|en|toilet water}} and {{m|en|toilet bag}}. This use is sometimes understood as a new borrowing from French, despite being the older sense of the English word. Medical jargon also includes some set phrases such as {{m|en|pulmonary toilet}} and {{m|en|toilet of the mouth}}; in both cases the word ''toilet'' means general hygiene.

